parts:
- text: ''
thinking_content: "**Initial Assessment & Prioritization**\n\nI've just finished reviewing the `graphfwpca_ridge.py` code within the Research Counter, and I'm comparing it with my current SOTA approach (ID 1894). The `graphfwpca_ridge` method has some notable differences, especially the lack of ComBat in the embedding path, the graph construction method, the use of Euclidean distance, and the potential (though not explicit) use of feature weighting via highly variable genes. Based on my review, the name \"FWPCA\" is a little misleading.\n\nMy immediate priority is to adapt my SOTA pipeline for the upcoming \"Multi-Organ Generalization\" task. My SOTA pipeline, which utilizes log1p, HVG selection, FWPCA, PCA, ComBat, and a sophisticated graph construction method (Adaptive Residualization + DAQB), seems more complex than the baseline. \n\nThe three immediate action items are: 1) Refactor my SOTA implementation into a reusable function; 2) Make sure my pipeline can produce the 24-column CSV\
  \ output; and 3) have an implementation of the Praxis VI baseline ready to go.\n\n**Action: Creating a Reusable SOTA Function**\n\nMy next action is to create a reusable Python function for my SOTA pipeline (ID 1894) and save it in my private storage. This is crucial for modularity, easier adaptation, and future debugging. This will be the foundational component for further experimentation. I'll create a new file `storage/pioneer/pioneer_sota_pipeline.py` and name the function `integrate_pioneer_sota`.\n\nI'll include all the existing components of my pipeline, but package them within the new function. This is an essential step towards more efficient and streamlined work.\n"
